INT. RIVERBOAT COURTROOM - DAY

A county courtroom built into the old gambling deck of a paddle steamer, moored permanently at the levee. Brass chandeliers. Faded green carpet stamped with playing-card suits. Through the tall windows, the brown river slides past.

MARGUERITE VOSS, 40s, defense counsel, navy suit, reading glasses pushed into silver-black hair, stands at the lectern. At the other table, ELIAS THORNQUIST, 60, prosecutor, rumpled, a pocket watch chain across his vest, watches her with weary patience.

On the bench sits JUDGE PRUITT, 70, silver pompadour, half-moon spectacles, a gavel carved like a riverboat wheel.

BAILIFF KRAUSE, 30s, broad, stands by the door with folded hands. On the stand, ILSA BRANDVOLD, 50s, a dock clerk in her Sunday best, twists a handkerchief.

VOSS
Miss Brandvold, you've told this court you saw the lantern at ten o'clock.

BRANDVOLD
Yes, ma'am.

VOSS
And that you were certain of the hour.

BRANDVOLD
The ten o'clock whistle had blown. I always know by the whistle.

VOSS
The whistle at the lower landing.

BRANDVOLD
Yes.

VOSS
Which was taken out of service on the third of March.

Brandvold's handkerchief stops moving.

BRANDVOLD
Was it?

VOSS
The county ordinance is Exhibit Fourteen. The occurrence was the ninth.

THORNQUIST
(rising)
Your Honor, counsel is testifying.

PRUITT
Sit down, Elias. She's cross-examining. It's what the lady is paid for.

Thornquist sits. He smiles slightly, almost to himself.

VOSS
Miss Brandvold, was there a different whistle?

BRANDVOLD
The upriver one, maybe. From the cannery.

VOSS
Which blows at a quarter past.

BRANDVOLD
I may have been wrong about the time.

A murmur ripples through the gallery. Pruitt raps once.

PRUITT
Order. Mr. Krause, the doors.

Krause opens the doors; a breeze off the river lifts the papers on both tables.

VOSS
Miss Brandvold, I don't want to embarrass you.

BRANDVOLD
You haven't, ma'am. I'd rather be wrong out loud than right and keep quiet.

Voss takes off her glasses. She lets that settle.

VOSS
Nothing further.

PRUITT
Mr. Thornquist?

THORNQUIST
Just one, Judge. Miss Brandvold. Whatever the hour, the lantern. You saw it.

BRANDVOLD
I saw it.

THORNQUIST
Where?

BRANDVOLD
On the Hollis dock. Same as always.

THORNQUIST
Thank you. That's all.

VOSS
(rising)
Your Honor, in light of the testimony, the defense moves to dismiss.

THORNQUIST ^
(rising)
Your Honor, the state withdraws the second count.

VOSS
You're withdrawing?

THORNQUIST
I'd be a fool to hold a case together with a whistle that doesn't work.

PRUITT
Counsel, approach.

They approach the bench. Pruitt lowers his voice, but the room is small and the wood carries it.

PRUITT (CONT'D)
Marguerite, I've watched you take apart three of Elias's cases this term.

VOSS
Thank you, Judge.

PRUITT
It wasn't a compliment. He lets you.

THORNQUIST
That's a lie, Walter.

PRUITT
It's Your Honor in my courtroom.

THORNQUIST
It's a boat, Walter.

Pruitt sighs and rubs the bridge of his nose.

PRUITT
Count two is dismissed. Count one will be heard Thursday. Mr. Krause, please escort Miss Brandvold out by the forward stairs.

Krause crosses to the stand and offers his arm. Brandvold takes it, shaky.

BRANDVOLD
I hope I didn't do harm.

KRAUSE
No, ma'am. You told the truth. That's the job.

They exit. Pruitt gathers his robes and steps down. Voss and Thornquist remain, packing files.

THORNQUIST
Dinner at the Delta Queen?

VOSS
You're buying.

THORNQUIST
I'm always buying.

VOSS
That's because you always lose.

THORNQUIST
No. That's because I like the company.

She laughs, finally. Together they walk out into the afternoon, past the dealers' cages now stripped to their brass.

The river keeps moving.
